<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="53576" href="schedule/2016/fall/CMN/529/53576.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2016" href="schedule/2016.xml">2016</calendarYear><term id="120168" href="schedule/2016/fall.xml">Fall 2016</term><subject id="CMN" href="schedule/2016/fall/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="529" href="schedule/2016/fall/CMN/529.xml">Seminar Communication Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>1  </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Interpersonal Influence</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>We will examine different theoretical frameworks related to influencing others in interpersonal encounters (e.g., interaction goals, social control strategies) and varying relational and goal outcomes related to these strategies. We will explore a variety of contexts in which individuals attempt to influence others, spanning topics (e.g., health, work), relationship types (e.g., parent-child, supervisor-subordinate), and motivations.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2016-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2016-12-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="S1 ">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>M      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4007</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Guntzviller" firstName="L">Guntzviller, L</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>